WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:23.523
Today, we are talking about
bonding between molecules.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:27.660
Now, we have talked a lot
about bonding within molecules.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.062
Right.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.563
And that got us to
saying things like,

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:32.232
well you can use
electronegativity,

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:33.366
You can use electronegativity,
for example, to say,

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.201
I got an ionic bond.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.503
All right.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:38.571
Or I got a non-polar
covalent bond

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:40.073
or I've got a polar
covalent bond.

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:41.975
But today, what
we're going to do

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:46.980
is talk about how
these molecules--

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.949
how these molecules
bond together.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.786
What sorts of interactions
can they have?

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:54.120
And those are really important.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.188
Right.

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:56.856
So that's going to be the topic.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:01:01.261
Now, if we just get started with
what we know what's up there.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:01.761
All right.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:02.962
Well, there's ionic.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.364
So listen, I'm going
to fill this in.

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:07.167
Bond, a model.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.134
You'll see what I mean.

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:09.836
I'm going to draw
a little cartoon.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:12.305
The attraction,
how it's attracted,

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:13.807
the energy range,
and an example.

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:14.307
Right.

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:18.812
So for an ionic bond,
we've got ionic--

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.447
ionic.

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:21.414
OK.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:22.549
And here we go.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:23.416
Watch this.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:28.254
Plus and minus--
there's my model.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:29.021
OK.

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:31.925
And the attraction here--
we'll get a little specific.

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:35.895
It's a cation and an anion.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:39.799
And we know that that's going
to go like Coulomb's Law.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:40.265
Right.

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:44.137
So the attraction, the energy,
not the force, the energy

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:48.541
will go like 1 over R, roughly.

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:49.542
Now, what is the energy?

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.978
Well, the energy is
pretty wide ranging.

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:56.850
I'm going to do this
in kilojoules per mole,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.218
since those are
the units I have.

00:01:58.218 --> 00:01:59.384
I'll write that in a second.

00:01:59.384 --> 00:02:03.490
And an ionic bond can have
lots and lots of range in here.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.458
And it typically
can be very strong.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.026
Something we've
already talked about.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:11.764
An example would be sodium
chloride, right there.

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.333
Let's put the units here.

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:15.568
The energy units are in--

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:16.736
I'm going to write it here--

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:19.172
kilojoules per mole.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:23.977
Now, in a covalent bond--

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:26.513
so covalent wouldn't be here.

00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:27.013
OK.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.049
So in a covalent
bond, remember, we've

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.452
got something very different.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.988
We've got this carrying
a sharing kind of thing,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.190
and we've got maybe two atoms.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:37.757
Right.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.025
Two atoms like this.

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:40.927
And then the charge is sort of--

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.930
the negative charge is sort
of floating around and sharing

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:48.668
those two centers,
like in H2 or Cl2.

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:51.838
I'm going to use Cl, so let's
keep using Cl, all right.

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:55.175
That would also be an
example of a covalent bond.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:03:01.347
And the energy range there is
also quite large, 150 to 1100

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:03.983
are the numbers I got.

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:12.025
The attraction is shared
electrons-- shared pair

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:14.627
of electrons.

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:18.531
And I'm not putting
an R dependence,

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:22.368
because for covalent
bond, it's complicated.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:23.937
It's complicated.

00:03:23.937 --> 00:03:24.504
There is no.

00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:25.471
It is.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:28.841
It's a complicated-- but it's a
very nice, happy relationship.

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:30.777
But it's a complicated
R dependence.

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:34.280
So you can't simplify it
like you can with a 1 over R

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.015
or like some of the
other interactions

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:37.917
we'll talk about today.

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:39.352
OK.

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:41.921
1 over R, not for covalent.

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:42.422
All right.

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:44.724
Now that's where we've been.

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:46.659
Mm.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.027
Mm.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:49.963
Here we go.

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:50.930
Here we go.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.131
Look at this.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.601
I'm not even-- I'm
just getting started.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:57.637
Because the thing
is, now, we got

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:59.906
to talking about how
these molecules can

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:00.807
talk to each other.

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.742
And there's a variety of ways.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:07.180
What I want to focus on
first is this thing here.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:10.650
That's that polar covalent
bond that we've already

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.284
talked about.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:13.987
So we've already got
a grounding for it.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:15.455
Right.

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:17.457
But I want to remind
you of what happens.

00:04:17.457 --> 00:04:17.957
Right.

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:20.493
So if I have, let's
keep with HCl.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:23.763
If I have HCl,
that's a nice version

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.932
of a polar covalent bond.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:29.202
And remember what happens
there is you've got

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.704
a sharing but sort of unfair.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:32.205
Right.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:36.042
So one of the atoms took a
little more of the charge.

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:38.611
And so sometimes
we represent that

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:41.014
with like a more of
the cloud, right,

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:42.882
on one side than the other.

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:44.550
But it's still a covalent bond.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.051
Yeah.

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:46.586
But the thing is
that-- and so what

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:48.288
you're left with
like in this case

00:04:48.288 --> 00:04:51.924
is some positive charge
on the hydrogen atom

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:54.127
and some negative charge
on the chlorine atom.

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.128
And we did this already.

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:57.230
We used these symbols.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.799
And we also talked
about how that leads you

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.868
to a dipole moment.

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:07.273
What is a dipole?

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:12.578
Well, a dipole is
just the description

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.080
of what you have
in this case, where

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.451
you've got two charges of
opposite signs separated

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.286
by some distance.

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:20.887
OK.

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.488
Two charges.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:23.756
Those aren't full charges.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:25.091
Those are partial charges.

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:25.591
Right.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:28.127
And they're separated by
some distance, the distance

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:29.629
between these two atoms.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.597
And you get a dipole moment.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:35.234
And it has a magnitude
and a direction.

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:38.738
And remember, just
to remind you,

00:05:38.738 --> 00:05:41.841
I did share this with you
in a previous lecture.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:45.478
But, you know, mu
equals the charge

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:47.413
times the distance
between the charge.

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:47.914
Right.

00:05:47.914 --> 00:05:50.582
So that would be like the
charge would be the deltas here

00:05:50.582 --> 00:05:53.286
and the distance between
them, right, would be this.

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:55.521
Right. 'r'.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:58.124
Now, remember the
units are in Debye.

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:06.799
So for this molecule, for
here, mu is around 1.8D.

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:16.909
And 1D is equal to 3.3 times
10 to the minus 30th Coulomb

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:17.410
meters.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.679
Those are the units.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:21.147
Charge times distance.

00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:22.248
Charge times distance.

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:22.915
Right.

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:23.416
OK.

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:27.186
3.3 times 10 to the minus
30th Coulomb meter is a Debye.

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:29.922
And again, just to remind you,
because we did talk about this

00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:32.358
when we talked about
polar covalent bonds,

00:06:32.358 --> 00:06:34.727
the dipole moment
of these molecules

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.163
tends to range from 0--

00:06:37.163 --> 00:06:39.565
mm-- right there.

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:42.769
Right, no dipole, to 10 or so--

00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:43.703
11.

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:44.904
Right.

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:46.305
Debyes.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:49.475
Now, why is this important?

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:53.713
Because if you now think,
well, what would happen?

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:57.683
What would happen if I
brought another charge up

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.352
to the dipole?

00:06:59.352 --> 00:07:00.119
What would happen?

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:00.620
Right.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.622
Let's say that I bring
a positive charge.

00:07:02.622 --> 00:07:05.224
Let's say that I'm
a sodium and I've

00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:08.394
lost that outer electron, which
we know is pretty easy to lose,

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:09.495
if you're sodium.

00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:09.996
Right.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:13.332
So I'm positively in charged,
and I'm coming along,

00:07:13.332 --> 00:07:15.268
and I see this dipole.

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:17.970
Well, it turns out that,
as you can imagine,

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:21.040
I'm going to be attracted
to that negative charge.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:21.641
Right.

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:25.545
So this molecule's going
to orient in such a way

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:32.351
that I can actually
bond to the molecule

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.754
and be attracted to the
dipole of the molecule.

00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:37.123
So that is actually
another type of bond.

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:37.623
Right.

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:41.594
And we'll call that ion-dipole.

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:44.330
It's an ion-dipole bond.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:45.832
OK.

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:51.103
Let's do this so that I
stay within the lines.

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:51.604
All right.

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:52.238
Now, OK.

00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:53.406
So what does that look like?

00:07:53.406 --> 00:07:55.975
Well, we just said
it's a charge.

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:59.145
I'm taking a single
charge, and I'm bringing it

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:02.548
to this thing that has a
shift of the charge within it

00:08:02.548 --> 00:08:04.350
that led to these
two partial charges.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.285
So, as you as you
can imagine, it's

00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:08.988
going to want to
orient to form a bond.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:10.556
All right, so let's
draw it that way.

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:11.057
All right.

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:13.860
So maybe if I were
to take HCl, maybe I

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.130
would have sort of like,
you know, a cloud like that.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:18.631
Right.

00:08:18.631 --> 00:08:21.868
Where it had it's a minus
and it's plus there.

00:08:21.868 --> 00:08:22.702
OK.

00:08:22.702 --> 00:08:23.603
Why did I--

00:08:23.603 --> 00:08:27.673
I don't know why used dash
lines, but there you go.

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:29.775
And so you can see that is--

00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:31.477
what I wanted to put
is the dashed line

00:08:31.477 --> 00:08:33.111
for the bond itself.

00:08:33.111 --> 00:08:35.881
Give myself a little space.

00:08:35.881 --> 00:08:37.350
Right.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:39.051
That's the bond
we're talking about.

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:40.352
That's the bond we're talking--

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:43.489
Now, it turns out that
this is an important type

00:08:43.489 --> 00:08:46.259
of interaction between
molecules and ions.

00:08:46.259 --> 00:08:47.894
It's an ion dipole interaction.

00:08:47.894 --> 00:08:49.829
And it has a range--

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:51.330
I wrote it down.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:09:01.908
It has a range of 40 to
600, huge range, 40 to 600.

00:09:01.908 --> 00:09:05.244
And the kind of
attraction is really not

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:08.748
describable in any other way
than what we just described.

00:09:08.748 --> 00:09:10.816
It's an ion and a dipole.

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:14.053
And it goes as 1 over r squared.

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:18.691
If you look at the separation,
the distance between these,

00:09:18.691 --> 00:09:23.663
right, that distance-- the
dependence on the energy,

00:09:23.663 --> 00:09:26.566
on the bond energy, will
go like that distance,

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:29.135
like, 1 over that
distance squared.

00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:32.138
Now, these dependencies
you can get from fairly

00:09:32.138 --> 00:09:33.506
simple electrostatics.

00:09:33.506 --> 00:09:35.007
I won't derive any of it.

00:09:35.007 --> 00:09:37.009
I'm telling you what it is.

00:09:37.009 --> 00:09:37.610
OK.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:38.110
Good.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:39.545
So an ion double--
now an example

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:43.049
of that would be the
system that I just drew.

00:09:43.049 --> 00:09:43.549
Right.

00:09:43.549 --> 00:09:50.156
So Na+ plus and HCl.

00:09:50.156 --> 00:09:51.023
OK.

00:09:51.023 --> 00:09:51.524
Good.

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:52.858
That's a different kind of bond.

00:09:52.858 --> 00:09:55.261
But see, now, I could
also have two dipoles.

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:57.997
And I got a picture for that.

00:09:57.997 --> 00:09:58.864
So here's a dipole.

00:09:58.864 --> 00:10:00.499
There's my HCl
dipole, and there it

00:10:00.499 --> 00:10:02.101
is with the negative
partial charge

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:03.369
and a positive partial charge.

00:10:03.369 --> 00:10:05.037
And it's also color coded.

00:10:05.037 --> 00:10:05.538
Right.

00:10:05.538 --> 00:10:06.038
OK.

00:10:06.038 --> 00:10:06.806
So that's cool.

00:10:06.806 --> 00:10:08.407
And there they are
just floating along.

00:10:08.407 --> 00:10:10.810
And I put an electric field on.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.678
And I put an electric field on.

00:10:12.678 --> 00:10:14.814
And you can see that
with the electric field--

00:10:14.814 --> 00:10:17.049
so there's my positive plate
and my negative plate--

00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:18.584
they're going to try to align.

00:10:18.584 --> 00:10:19.452
Right.

00:10:19.452 --> 00:10:22.788
These dipoles mean that they're
going to align with the field.

00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:24.357
They're going to
align with the field.

00:10:24.357 --> 00:10:27.159
So all of those
positive edges are

00:10:27.159 --> 00:10:29.128
going to try to turn and
face the negative one.

00:10:29.128 --> 00:10:31.464
And all those negative ones
are going to turn and face

00:10:31.464 --> 00:10:33.332
the positive one.

00:10:33.332 --> 00:10:37.069
And that's exactly what
we just did with this ion.

00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:38.638
We basically did
the same thing here.

00:10:38.638 --> 00:10:40.406
That's what the ion did.

00:10:40.406 --> 00:10:40.906
OK.

00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:41.907
That's what the ion did.

00:10:41.907 --> 00:10:45.144
It said, hey, come and face
me and let's form a bond.

00:10:45.144 --> 00:10:49.315
But these can also be
attracted to each other.

00:10:49.315 --> 00:10:49.815
All right.

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:51.417
And so I've got a picture there.

00:10:51.417 --> 00:10:55.855
So you can see, like, if I have
a dipole in another dipole--

00:10:55.855 --> 00:10:58.124
so this is not an external
field, and it's not an ion.

00:10:58.124 --> 00:11:02.895
It's two dipoles,
two HCl molecules.

00:11:02.895 --> 00:11:03.562
Right.

00:11:03.562 --> 00:11:06.432
Well, they each have a plus
charge and a minus charge.

00:11:06.432 --> 00:11:09.035
They each have a plus charge and
a minus charge and, you know,

00:11:09.035 --> 00:11:13.039
partial charge,
right, polar covalent.

00:11:13.039 --> 00:11:15.341
And you can see that those
can line up, actually,

00:11:15.341 --> 00:11:17.977
in different ways
to form attraction.

00:11:17.977 --> 00:11:20.079
And they can also repel.

00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:20.813
Right.

00:11:20.813 --> 00:11:23.149
But overall, when they get
together, or when two of them

00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:25.351
get together, they're going
to find a way to attract,

00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:26.318
because they can--

00:11:26.318 --> 00:11:27.687
because they can.

00:11:27.687 --> 00:11:33.192
And so that is another kind
of inter-molecular attraction,

00:11:33.192 --> 00:11:35.761
and it's called a dipole-dipole.

00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:38.831
Dipole-dipole.

00:11:38.831 --> 00:11:41.033
But, let's draw this.

00:11:45.037 --> 00:11:49.208
And if we do that we have--

00:11:49.208 --> 00:11:49.975
let's see.

00:11:49.975 --> 00:11:52.178
I'm going to try to draw this.

00:11:52.178 --> 00:11:54.747
So I've got one dipole.

00:11:54.747 --> 00:11:56.348
I'm going to stick
to that picture--

00:11:56.348 --> 00:11:57.983
oh, I'm going to
stick to this picture,

00:11:57.983 --> 00:11:59.719
but it's the same
as this picture,

00:11:59.719 --> 00:12:02.722
right, where I'm actually
using the kind of shape

00:12:02.722 --> 00:12:05.791
here to show you that
more charge has gone over

00:12:05.791 --> 00:12:07.126
to one side.

00:12:07.126 --> 00:12:07.626
OK.

00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:08.928
So let's go back to here.

00:12:08.928 --> 00:12:09.428
Right.

00:12:09.428 --> 00:12:11.764
And so if that happens,
then I've got

00:12:11.764 --> 00:12:14.734
minus and plus, thank you.

00:12:14.734 --> 00:12:15.468
OK.

00:12:15.468 --> 00:12:18.104
And then I'm going to have
another one that's going

00:12:18.104 --> 00:12:21.006
to want to line up like this.

00:12:21.006 --> 00:12:21.774
Oh.

00:12:21.774 --> 00:12:22.708
Did I do that right?

00:12:22.708 --> 00:12:23.509
Yeah.

00:12:23.509 --> 00:12:25.111
And this is the bond.

00:12:25.111 --> 00:12:27.346
This dashed line here
is the kind of bond

00:12:27.346 --> 00:12:28.914
that we're talking about.

00:12:28.914 --> 00:12:33.185
Yeah, those are almost
sort of asymmetric.

00:12:33.185 --> 00:12:34.854
OK.

00:12:34.854 --> 00:12:36.088
Now-- gesundheit.

00:12:36.088 --> 00:12:37.389
Now, that's a dipole-dipole.

00:12:37.389 --> 00:12:39.759
We don't really have a better
way of calling that either.

00:12:39.759 --> 00:12:42.361
They're dipole
charges interacting

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:44.396
with dipole charges.

00:12:44.396 --> 00:12:49.301
And that's going to give you
a 1 over r cubed dependence.

00:12:49.301 --> 00:12:52.671
And, you know, if you've taken
some basic ENM or if you do,

00:12:52.671 --> 00:12:53.539
you'll learn this.

00:12:53.539 --> 00:12:54.039
Right.

00:12:54.039 --> 00:12:56.709
You'll learn how to derive
these distance dependencies.

00:12:56.709 --> 00:13:01.380
So I've got two dipoles,
right, because there are

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.283
two molecules that have not--

00:13:04.283 --> 00:13:05.451
is not a non-polar.

00:13:05.451 --> 00:13:07.019
It's a polar covalent bond.

00:13:07.019 --> 00:13:09.088
And those are attracted
to each other in this way.

00:13:09.088 --> 00:13:12.525
And the range of that bonding
strength for two dipoles

00:13:12.525 --> 00:13:14.593
is 5 to 25.

00:13:14.593 --> 00:13:16.362
So it's a lot less.

00:13:16.362 --> 00:13:17.229
Right.

00:13:17.229 --> 00:13:23.669
And well, if you had HCl and
HCl, that would be an example--

00:13:23.669 --> 00:13:29.642
now instead of an ion, I'm
putting two polar molecules in.

00:13:29.642 --> 00:13:30.176
Right.

00:13:30.176 --> 00:13:34.380
So this could happen.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:40.286
This could happen from a
molecule to a molecule.

00:13:40.286 --> 00:13:42.922
It could happen from
an atom to a molecule.

00:13:42.922 --> 00:13:46.392
It could happen from
a molecule to an atom.

00:13:46.392 --> 00:13:47.793
Right.

00:13:47.793 --> 00:13:51.463
So for example, you
know, I could have--

00:13:51.463 --> 00:14:01.574
if I have HCl, and more charge
has gone to the chlorine side.

00:14:01.574 --> 00:14:02.074
Right.

00:14:02.074 --> 00:14:03.375
So this has my delta plus.

00:14:03.375 --> 00:14:05.211
This is what I wrote
over on the other side.

00:14:05.211 --> 00:14:07.012
That's my delta minus.

00:14:07.012 --> 00:14:08.814
And if I have just
a single atom--

00:14:08.814 --> 00:14:11.016
I'm going to put
a xenon atom here.

00:14:11.016 --> 00:14:13.786
Well, it's just
sitting around happily,

00:14:13.786 --> 00:14:16.589
you know, minding
it's own business.

00:14:16.589 --> 00:14:18.357
And what's going
to happen to that?

00:14:18.357 --> 00:14:19.058
Right.

00:14:19.058 --> 00:14:20.359
What's going to happen to that?

00:14:20.359 --> 00:14:25.464
Well, see, if this
comes up to an atom that

00:14:25.464 --> 00:14:29.969
didn't have a dipole,
it still has feelings.

00:14:29.969 --> 00:14:31.136
It still has feelings.

00:14:31.136 --> 00:14:31.737
Why?

00:14:31.737 --> 00:14:34.573
Because electrons have feelings.

00:14:34.573 --> 00:14:35.374
That's why.

00:14:35.374 --> 00:14:38.611
Because it has electrons.

00:14:38.611 --> 00:14:43.382
So those electrons are in there,
and it doesn't have a dipole.

00:14:43.382 --> 00:14:48.420
But that's OK, because those
electrons still can feel.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.757
They can feel the
dipole of this.

00:14:51.757 --> 00:14:56.595
They can feel the
dipole of this molecule.

00:14:56.595 --> 00:14:59.698
And when they feel that
dipole, they react to it.

00:14:59.698 --> 00:15:01.100
They react to it.

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:03.068
So there's a whole other
thing that can happen.

00:15:03.068 --> 00:15:04.703
By the way, it could happen--

00:15:04.703 --> 00:15:07.406
it could happen
with a sodium atom.

00:15:07.406 --> 00:15:13.946
Na+ also is a charge that
this happy go lucky xenon,

00:15:13.946 --> 00:15:16.248
it doesn't know what's coming.

00:15:16.248 --> 00:15:19.985
But it's got electrons
that feel charge.

00:15:19.985 --> 00:15:22.187
And so you bring
in charge to it,

00:15:22.187 --> 00:15:24.456
and they're going
to react to it.

00:15:24.456 --> 00:15:25.958
They're going to react to it.

00:15:25.958 --> 00:15:29.028
Whether it's a single charge
or whether it's a dipole,

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:32.965
these are charges that
are going to change

00:15:32.965 --> 00:15:35.801
the charge around that atom.

00:15:35.801 --> 00:15:38.103
Now, there's another
name for that.

00:15:38.103 --> 00:15:40.706
And that leads to
another kind of bond.

00:15:40.706 --> 00:15:43.375
It actually leads to
another kind of bond.

00:15:43.375 --> 00:15:44.143
Let's see.

00:15:44.143 --> 00:15:45.344
So I'm going to just--

00:15:45.344 --> 00:15:46.645
let me draw the cartoon.

00:15:46.645 --> 00:15:48.380
I really want to make
sure you understand

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:52.351
this, because this sets up
the next kind of bonding.

00:15:52.351 --> 00:15:59.091
And so let's suppose I take
a sodium, and I'll use H2.

00:15:59.091 --> 00:16:00.960
H-- gesundheit.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.562
H. And mine could have
been a single atom.

00:16:03.562 --> 00:16:06.398
It could be a
non-polar molecule.

00:16:06.398 --> 00:16:06.899
Right.

00:16:06.899 --> 00:16:10.536
So this is what this looks like.

00:16:10.536 --> 00:16:14.606
And now this is going to--

00:16:14.606 --> 00:16:16.275
these are kind of
walking along happily,

00:16:16.275 --> 00:16:17.876
and then they see each other.

00:16:17.876 --> 00:16:20.212
And all of a sudden,
this is going

00:16:20.212 --> 00:16:24.683
to induce a shift of the
charge around that H2 molecule.

00:16:24.683 --> 00:16:28.721
So what you're going
to see is now more--

00:16:28.721 --> 00:16:31.156
which way is it going to go?

00:16:31.156 --> 00:16:33.659
Which should have the bigger
side, the more charge?

00:16:33.659 --> 00:16:34.760
Right, yeah, here.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.962
Because that's positive,
and electrons are negative.

00:16:36.962 --> 00:16:40.632
So now it's going
to look like this.

00:16:40.632 --> 00:16:42.901
Now, that was not a dipole.

00:16:42.901 --> 00:16:44.870
It is not a molecule
with a dipole.

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:50.309
But it's an induced
dipole, induced dipole,

00:16:50.309 --> 00:16:52.778
because of feelings.

00:16:52.778 --> 00:16:54.513
It comes down to feelings.

00:16:54.513 --> 00:16:55.614
That has a name.

00:16:55.614 --> 00:16:56.115
All right.

00:16:56.115 --> 00:16:57.149
That's ion.

00:16:57.149 --> 00:17:00.686
that one right there
is ion-induced--

00:17:00.686 --> 00:17:02.454
see if I can fit it all here--

00:17:02.454 --> 00:17:06.558
induced dipole.

00:17:06.558 --> 00:17:07.893
Ion-induced dipole.

00:17:07.893 --> 00:17:09.528
And I'm going to try
to draw this now.

00:17:09.528 --> 00:17:10.229
Let's see.

00:17:10.229 --> 00:17:11.163
OK.

00:17:11.163 --> 00:17:13.432
Hang on.

00:17:13.432 --> 00:17:13.932
OK.

00:17:13.932 --> 00:17:16.902
So the ion-induced dipole--

00:17:16.902 --> 00:17:19.405
I'm going to have
a plus charge here,

00:17:19.405 --> 00:17:21.205
and here's what I'm going to do.

00:17:21.205 --> 00:17:28.414
I'm going to draw the
original shape as what it was.

00:17:28.414 --> 00:17:29.715
Oh, boy.

00:17:29.715 --> 00:17:30.582
Here we go.

00:17:30.582 --> 00:17:31.817
That's what it was.

00:17:31.817 --> 00:17:33.152
But then it got induced.

00:17:36.455 --> 00:17:37.289
OK.

00:17:37.289 --> 00:17:38.624
It got induced.

00:17:38.624 --> 00:17:40.659
And so then, once
it's induced, you've

00:17:40.659 --> 00:17:44.596
got like a minus and a plus.

00:17:44.596 --> 00:17:46.832
Happens all the time.

00:17:46.832 --> 00:17:49.001
It happens all the time.

00:17:49.001 --> 00:17:52.504
Now, the way that it will--
we'll talk about this

00:17:52.504 --> 00:17:53.939
in a little bit of
a different way,

00:17:53.939 --> 00:17:58.343
because this attraction
is between an ion--

00:17:58.343 --> 00:18:01.447
by the way, an ion just means an
atom or something lost a charge

00:18:01.447 --> 00:18:03.982
or has a charge, plus or minus.

00:18:03.982 --> 00:18:07.219
Remember, cation anion.

00:18:07.219 --> 00:18:11.490
But the ion in this case
is interacting with what

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:14.526
is called a polarizable--

00:18:14.526 --> 00:18:19.565
polarizable electron cloud.

00:18:19.565 --> 00:18:22.868
Now, the electron
cloud is nothing new.

00:18:22.868 --> 00:18:24.536
We got an electron cloud nailed.

00:18:24.536 --> 00:18:26.271
We know about electron cloud.

00:18:26.271 --> 00:18:26.805
Right.

00:18:26.805 --> 00:18:29.741
Because those are orbitals.

00:18:29.741 --> 00:18:32.211
But what is this thing
called polarizable?

00:18:32.211 --> 00:18:33.178
Right, polarizable.

00:18:33.178 --> 00:18:34.646
And that's a very
important term.

00:18:34.646 --> 00:18:39.351
So I'm going to write
down its definition.

00:18:39.351 --> 00:18:44.656
Because you can
imagine if I have

00:18:44.656 --> 00:18:47.559
a way of being attracted
to a non-polar molecule

00:18:47.559 --> 00:18:51.997
by shifting its charge, by
shifting its cloud, then

00:18:51.997 --> 00:18:53.732
how easy was that?

00:18:53.732 --> 00:18:55.300
How much could I shift it?

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:57.736
You can tell that that's going
to affect the bond strength.

00:18:57.736 --> 00:18:59.638
By the way, which is--

00:18:59.638 --> 00:19:04.476
let's see-- 3 to 15
and has a 1 over r

00:19:04.476 --> 00:19:08.213
to the fourth dependence.

00:19:08.213 --> 00:19:10.849
And let's give us-- let's give
the example that I gave there.

00:19:10.849 --> 00:19:15.654
Na+ and H, H.

00:19:15.654 --> 00:19:17.256
OK, non-polar molecule.

00:19:17.256 --> 00:19:17.756
OK.

00:19:17.756 --> 00:19:19.458
It could have been an atom.

00:19:19.458 --> 00:19:23.228
But It could have been
a non-polar molecule.

00:19:23.228 --> 00:19:23.996
Yeah.

00:19:23.996 --> 00:19:27.132
Now, polarizable--
polarizability,

00:19:27.132 --> 00:19:29.034
is a very important concept.

00:19:29.034 --> 00:19:35.541
Polarizability is the measure--

00:19:38.177 --> 00:20:01.200
gesundheit-- Of how easy it
is to temporarily distort

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:03.168
the electron distribution.

00:20:09.508 --> 00:20:13.779
That is a very important
concept for these types of bonds

00:20:13.779 --> 00:20:16.915
and for a lot of other
properties and, of course,

00:20:16.915 --> 00:20:17.983
for life in general.

00:20:17.983 --> 00:20:20.419
How polarizable
are my electrons?

00:20:20.419 --> 00:20:21.153
I need to know.

00:20:23.822 --> 00:20:28.393
Are they just willing to deform
from their happy orbitals?

00:20:28.393 --> 00:20:32.030
Temporarily,
because if they were

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:33.865
going to deform
permanently, then you'd

00:20:33.865 --> 00:20:36.101
have a dipole, a
permanent dipole.

00:20:36.101 --> 00:20:38.503
These are induced dipoles.

00:20:38.503 --> 00:20:39.271
OK.

00:20:39.271 --> 00:20:41.607
That's important.

00:20:41.607 --> 00:20:43.275
OK.

00:20:43.275 --> 00:20:46.278
Now, OK, good.

00:20:46.278 --> 00:20:47.879
So that's an ion-induced dipole.

00:20:47.879 --> 00:20:50.449
Now you could also
imagine, you know,

00:20:50.449 --> 00:20:53.952
this could have easily
been a dipole over here.

00:20:53.952 --> 00:20:56.321
It didn't have to be an ion.

00:20:56.321 --> 00:20:57.489
It could have been a dipole.

00:20:57.489 --> 00:20:59.091
I could have had
a dipole here that

00:20:59.091 --> 00:21:03.762
came up to a non-polar
molecule or an atom, right,

00:21:03.762 --> 00:21:09.134
and it could have
induced the same shift

00:21:09.134 --> 00:21:12.237
in the charge density, which
then allows me to be attracted,

00:21:12.237 --> 00:21:14.106
because there's a little
bit of a fluctuation

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:15.807
of negative charge there.

00:21:15.807 --> 00:21:17.476
Right.

00:21:17.476 --> 00:21:18.443
So if I were to--

00:21:22.147 --> 00:21:27.052
so the thing is, we've
been talking about dimers.

00:21:27.052 --> 00:21:31.089
But you could talk about, what
is the dipole of something

00:21:31.089 --> 00:21:32.024
more than a dimer.

00:21:32.024 --> 00:21:33.759
So this is polar.

00:21:33.759 --> 00:21:34.993
What about these other things?

00:21:34.993 --> 00:21:35.861
Right.

00:21:35.861 --> 00:21:40.799
So if I look at molecules,
right, that are polar,

00:21:40.799 --> 00:21:44.436
it's not just about
HCl, right, which

00:21:44.436 --> 00:21:46.138
is that first one up there.

00:21:46.138 --> 00:21:48.840
But that's kind of
the obvious case.

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:50.575
But what about CCl4?

00:21:50.575 --> 00:21:53.612
What if I take a carbon
atom and I put 4 chlorines?

00:21:53.612 --> 00:21:54.846
Those are all polar bonds.

00:21:54.846 --> 00:21:57.115
And you can see-- well, it's
hard to see-- but they've

00:21:57.115 --> 00:21:59.818
drawn in the dipole moment.

00:21:59.818 --> 00:22:01.620
You've lost some
charge from the carbon.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:03.388
It's gone out to
the chlorine atom.

00:22:03.388 --> 00:22:05.424
There's a dipole movement
within each bond.

00:22:05.424 --> 00:22:08.327
But now here's where
VSEPR comes in,

00:22:08.327 --> 00:22:14.800
because these are going to
be tetrahedrally distributed

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:17.102
and fully canceling out.

00:22:17.102 --> 00:22:18.704
So it's like you've
got all these kind

00:22:18.704 --> 00:22:22.040
of electrostatic things
going on that all cancel out.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:24.743
So this molecule is
not a polar molecule.

00:22:24.743 --> 00:22:25.344
Right.

00:22:25.344 --> 00:22:30.782
On the other hand,
molecules NH3 or CH3Cl--

00:22:30.782 --> 00:22:31.249
right.

00:22:31.249 --> 00:22:34.186
Now, this is going to be polar,
because these dipoles do not

00:22:34.186 --> 00:22:35.620
cancel out within the molecules.

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:37.189
So you can kind of
use this thinking.

00:22:37.189 --> 00:22:40.092
There's BF3, trigonal planar.

00:22:40.092 --> 00:22:41.159
Right.

00:22:41.159 --> 00:22:42.661
All those dipoles cancel.

00:22:42.661 --> 00:22:45.931
It's a non-polar molecule.

00:22:45.931 --> 00:22:47.899
But if you take a
classic example--

00:22:47.899 --> 00:22:49.534
which we'll come back--

00:22:49.534 --> 00:22:55.073
of, say, water as
a polar molecule,

00:22:55.073 --> 00:22:56.174
I could do the same thing.

00:22:56.174 --> 00:22:56.675
Right.

00:22:56.675 --> 00:23:01.446
I can take water, which
is oxygen, hydrogen,

00:23:01.446 --> 00:23:04.750
hydrogen. And you see you've
got delta plus there, delta

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:08.019
plus there, and you've
got some minus there.

00:23:08.019 --> 00:23:08.754
Right.

00:23:08.754 --> 00:23:14.793
Now, there's a dipole
moment, so part of it cancels

00:23:14.793 --> 00:23:17.162
but not all of it.

00:23:17.162 --> 00:23:21.867
So there is a dipole moment
for the net water molecule.

00:23:21.867 --> 00:23:24.469
The net dipole is what
matters to determine

00:23:24.469 --> 00:23:27.005
if a molecule's polar or not.

00:23:27.005 --> 00:23:30.709
So there's a net
dipole, net dipole

00:23:30.709 --> 00:23:32.077
when you sum them all up.

00:23:32.077 --> 00:23:33.645
And that's what's
going to determine

00:23:33.645 --> 00:23:37.215
if this can come along to
that xenon atom, right.

00:23:37.215 --> 00:23:42.220
And so here's my xenon just
sitting there doing nothing.

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:46.024
And now when water comes up to
xenon, what's going to happen?

00:23:46.024 --> 00:23:47.859
Well, what's going
to happen is I've

00:23:47.859 --> 00:23:53.198
got my water molecule, my
water molecule, my delta minus,

00:23:53.198 --> 00:23:55.200
my delta plus, my delta plus.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:59.171
And because it's
got a net dipole,

00:23:59.171 --> 00:24:01.540
it's going to form the atom.

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:03.508
And so I'm going to have--

00:24:03.508 --> 00:24:05.177
let's see, make sure
I draw this right.

00:24:05.177 --> 00:24:06.845
So it's going to look
kind of like this.

00:24:11.082 --> 00:24:16.388
Delta minus, delta plus, and
that allows a water molecule

00:24:16.388 --> 00:24:18.390
to bond to a xenon atom.

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:24.729
It is bonding with another
bond, which is very

00:24:24.729 --> 00:24:27.532
similar to what I just wrote--

00:24:27.532 --> 00:24:34.105
gesundheit-- But it is a
dipole-induced, not ion.

00:24:34.105 --> 00:24:39.611
It is a dipole-induced dipole.

00:24:39.611 --> 00:24:40.512
You see that?

00:24:40.512 --> 00:24:42.747
Because now, I
didn't have an ion.

00:24:42.747 --> 00:24:48.720
I had a water molecule
that had a dipole moment.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:50.989
So now I've got my
dipole-induced dipole,

00:24:50.989 --> 00:24:52.457
and that's going to look--

00:24:52.457 --> 00:24:53.458
oh let's draw it.

00:24:53.458 --> 00:24:53.959
Let's see.

00:24:53.959 --> 00:24:56.394
So I got my
dipole-induced dipole.

00:24:56.394 --> 00:25:01.233
So we're going to go, kind
of, oh, boy, there we go.

00:25:01.233 --> 00:25:02.667
OK.

00:25:02.667 --> 00:25:06.304
Minus, plus, and then--

00:25:06.304 --> 00:25:09.107
now that might be the
dipole of, say, the water

00:25:09.107 --> 00:25:10.242
molecule or something that.

00:25:10.242 --> 00:25:14.980
And then I've got another thing
that didn't have a dipole,

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:17.949
but it got induced to have one.

00:25:17.949 --> 00:25:20.652
OK.

00:25:20.652 --> 00:25:21.152
Oh, boy.

00:25:21.152 --> 00:25:23.421
I went out of the lines.

00:25:23.421 --> 00:25:24.356
Ah.

00:25:24.356 --> 00:25:25.891
I was so careful.

00:25:25.891 --> 00:25:28.560
But you can't-- you
can't hold chemistry.

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.495
Uh-uh.

00:25:30.495 --> 00:25:31.730
No way.

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:35.500
No way.

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:37.402
Mm.

00:25:37.402 --> 00:25:38.069
OK.

00:25:38.069 --> 00:25:42.040
Delta plus, delta
minus, it's induced.

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:44.476
That's what that means, right.

00:25:44.476 --> 00:25:48.780
Now, it turns out that
this has an even weaker--

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:49.848
so this would be a dipole.

00:25:49.848 --> 00:25:51.583
Let's just-- right,
let's finish this up--

00:25:51.583 --> 00:26:00.258
dipole interacting with the
polarizable electron cloud.

00:26:00.258 --> 00:26:07.265
And the value of this is
even lower range, 2 to 10,

00:26:07.265 --> 00:26:10.502
and the dependence is
1 over r to the sixth.

00:26:10.502 --> 00:26:15.006
And the example--
let's go back to HCl.

00:26:15.006 --> 00:26:19.978
HCl, there's my
dipole coming in ClCl.

00:26:19.978 --> 00:26:22.647
I like that because
it's got three Cls.

00:26:22.647 --> 00:26:23.448
Why not?

00:26:23.448 --> 00:26:25.383
That's a non-polar molecule.

00:26:25.383 --> 00:26:26.384
That's a dipole.

00:26:26.384 --> 00:26:29.054
That induces a dipole
in this by shifting

00:26:29.054 --> 00:26:31.590
its polarized electrons around.

00:26:31.590 --> 00:26:32.591
Right.

00:26:32.591 --> 00:26:36.094
And then it allows there to
be a little bit of attraction.

00:26:36.094 --> 00:26:38.563
You might be saying,
but that's so little.

00:26:38.563 --> 00:26:39.831
We'll get to that in a second.

00:26:39.831 --> 00:26:41.499
That's not so little.

00:26:41.499 --> 00:26:45.103
That's actually not so little
when you've got a lot of it.

00:26:45.103 --> 00:26:46.137
OK.

00:26:46.137 --> 00:26:48.006
OK.

00:26:48.006 --> 00:26:54.079
Dipole interaction-- now
it turns out that even--

00:26:54.079 --> 00:26:57.182
and this is what's mind blowing.

00:26:57.182 --> 00:26:58.183
You knew this was coming.

00:26:58.183 --> 00:27:01.086
This moment today
was going to come.

00:27:01.086 --> 00:27:07.192
I can have an interaction
without any dipole

00:27:07.192 --> 00:27:08.593
to start with.

00:27:08.593 --> 00:27:09.961
It's true.

00:27:09.961 --> 00:27:12.030
I can take two
non-polar covalent

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:16.501
bonds, two non-polar covalent
molecules, H2 and H2,

00:27:16.501 --> 00:27:20.805
and they are attracted
to each other.

00:27:20.805 --> 00:27:22.841
How is that possible?

00:27:22.841 --> 00:27:24.743
Well, it's what explains this.

00:27:24.743 --> 00:27:25.877
I mean, we know--

00:27:25.877 --> 00:27:28.079
why does helium,
argon, and xenon--

00:27:28.079 --> 00:27:30.115
why do these have
different boiling points?

00:27:30.115 --> 00:27:33.518
By the way, the boiling
point of these--

00:27:33.518 --> 00:27:35.820
all right, so these can
be made into liquids

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:38.523
and you can boil them, right--

00:27:38.523 --> 00:27:40.258
is really different.

00:27:40.258 --> 00:27:42.727
And it's a measure of
how strongly they're

00:27:42.727 --> 00:27:43.662
bonded together.

00:27:43.662 --> 00:27:44.162
Right.

00:27:44.162 --> 00:27:47.399
So this is a direct kind
of way of thinking about,

00:27:47.399 --> 00:27:50.168
of measuring something that's
related to the bond strength.

00:27:50.168 --> 00:27:52.203
So why do these atoms--

00:27:52.203 --> 00:27:55.306
they're all just atoms--

00:27:55.306 --> 00:27:59.511
you know, they're
all just like this.

00:27:59.511 --> 00:28:02.380
They don't have this dipole.

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:03.014
Right.

00:28:03.014 --> 00:28:03.782
They're non-polar.

00:28:03.782 --> 00:28:06.851
They're just atoms sitting there
with their symmetric charge

00:28:06.851 --> 00:28:07.452
clouds.

00:28:07.452 --> 00:28:08.753
How is it that they
can be attracted

00:28:08.753 --> 00:28:10.188
to each other in
the first place?

00:28:10.188 --> 00:28:14.693
And how is it that they can
have such a strong trend?

00:28:14.693 --> 00:28:18.263
And that has to do with
another kind of force.

00:28:18.263 --> 00:28:19.297
And there is a picture.

00:28:19.297 --> 00:28:21.399
So here's two examples.

00:28:21.399 --> 00:28:22.467
There's two helium atoms.

00:28:22.467 --> 00:28:25.070
So that's the first one in
this list, boiling point

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:27.072
minus 269 degrees Celsius.

00:28:27.072 --> 00:28:28.106
It's pretty cold.

00:28:28.106 --> 00:28:30.875
And there's two
non-polar molecules, H2.

00:28:30.875 --> 00:28:35.113
What happens is that
you get fluctuations.

00:28:35.113 --> 00:28:36.214
You get fluctuations.

00:28:36.214 --> 00:28:40.685
And what I mean by that is
that, just randomly, literally,

00:28:40.685 --> 00:28:45.290
randomly, right, because of
collisions or thermal energy--

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:49.828
randomly, one of those
could form a dipole.

00:28:49.828 --> 00:28:50.662
I didn't mean it.

00:28:50.662 --> 00:28:53.231
It wasn't my happy
place, but it happened.

00:28:53.231 --> 00:28:55.967
Now, if it happens and
your charge kind of

00:28:55.967 --> 00:29:00.371
goes to one side, even if
it's just for a split second,

00:29:00.371 --> 00:29:03.074
that's a dipole that then,
if you're near another atom,

00:29:03.074 --> 00:29:05.610
can induce a dipole.

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:09.114
And we know that if we can
induce a dipole, if we're here,

00:29:09.114 --> 00:29:11.850
if we're at this stage, we've
already got that covered.

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:15.987
We already know that I
can induce another dipole

00:29:15.987 --> 00:29:17.922
if I'm charged.

00:29:17.922 --> 00:29:18.656
Right.

00:29:18.656 --> 00:29:22.427
And so what happens is you
get this kind of fluctuation

00:29:22.427 --> 00:29:25.663
that leads literally to a bond.

00:29:25.663 --> 00:29:28.199
It leads to a bond.

00:29:28.199 --> 00:29:28.700
Right.

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:32.170
And that is called
London dispersion.

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.839
That's called the London force,
and the London dispersion

00:29:34.839 --> 00:29:38.576
is the name of a kind
of bonding you have.

00:29:38.576 --> 00:29:40.078
Let me just make sure--

00:29:40.078 --> 00:29:41.412
ha.

00:29:41.412 --> 00:29:43.815
OK.

00:29:43.815 --> 00:29:48.419
London dispersion.

00:29:54.058 --> 00:29:55.760
Notice there's
room for one more.

00:29:55.760 --> 00:29:56.728
It's coming.

00:29:56.728 --> 00:29:58.763
There's more.

00:29:58.763 --> 00:30:00.865
We're not done.

00:30:00.865 --> 00:30:01.933
We are not done.

00:30:01.933 --> 00:30:07.138
I can literally take two
non-polar atoms or molecules,

00:30:07.138 --> 00:30:12.010
and I can find a way for
them to be attracted.

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:15.580
And the way it happens
is thermal fluctuations.

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:20.952
This is such an important force,
and it's such a strange one

00:30:20.952 --> 00:30:24.189
that I want to write
down what it is.

00:30:24.189 --> 00:30:28.092
It's that non-polar-- we've
been talking about polar

00:30:28.092 --> 00:30:31.763
and charges, but
non-polar molecules

00:30:31.763 --> 00:30:34.999
are attracted by fluctuations.

00:30:43.174 --> 00:30:45.977
Fluctuations.

00:30:45.977 --> 00:30:48.313
I should say, by
dipole fluctuations.

00:30:48.313 --> 00:30:50.615
That's really what's happening.

00:30:50.615 --> 00:30:51.282
Right.

00:30:51.282 --> 00:30:52.750
By dipole fluctuations.

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:55.820
So one of them gets a little
shift, and there's a dipole.

00:30:55.820 --> 00:30:58.122
And then that dipole, if
there's another one around,

00:30:58.122 --> 00:31:02.093
can induce a shift, and
then there can be a bond.

00:31:02.093 --> 00:31:07.765
And now, this gets back to
something very important,

00:31:07.765 --> 00:31:10.568
which is that--

00:31:10.568 --> 00:31:16.507
you can imagine, how easy
was it for temperature--

00:31:16.507 --> 00:31:20.044
how easy was it to
create that shift?

00:31:20.044 --> 00:31:20.712
Right.

00:31:20.712 --> 00:31:25.483
Well that's going to have to
do with your polarizability.

00:31:25.483 --> 00:31:30.788
Now, OK, so what is it that
has to do with polarizability?

00:31:30.788 --> 00:31:31.289
Right.

00:31:31.289 --> 00:31:34.392
Well, if-- oh, I do
have a board here.

00:31:34.392 --> 00:31:36.461
Ha.

00:31:36.461 --> 00:31:36.961
OK.

00:31:40.732 --> 00:31:46.237
You can already imagine, if
I go back to this list here--

00:31:46.237 --> 00:31:49.073
if I go from helium to
argon, what have I done?

00:31:49.073 --> 00:31:51.142
I've gone down in
the periodic table.

00:31:51.142 --> 00:31:52.944
And we've already
talked about this.

00:31:52.944 --> 00:31:57.248
Those electrons that are going
to do the shifting to cause

00:31:57.248 --> 00:31:58.716
the fluctuation--

00:31:58.716 --> 00:32:02.387
those electrons are
more loosely bound.

00:32:02.387 --> 00:32:02.887
Right.

00:32:02.887 --> 00:32:04.656
They're further out.

00:32:04.656 --> 00:32:06.157
They're more loosely bound.

00:32:06.157 --> 00:32:07.492
We've already talked about this.

00:32:07.492 --> 00:32:09.193
We've looked at this
in a lot of ways.

00:32:09.193 --> 00:32:09.694
Right.

00:32:09.694 --> 00:32:13.564
Whether it's radius or
ionization energy, those are--

00:32:13.564 --> 00:32:16.734
if they're more loosely
bound, then they're

00:32:16.734 --> 00:32:18.536
easier to shift around.

00:32:18.536 --> 00:32:19.837
I'm not taking them out.

00:32:19.837 --> 00:32:20.338
Right.

00:32:20.338 --> 00:32:21.406
I'm not taking them out.

00:32:21.406 --> 00:32:22.807
But I'm moving them.

00:32:22.807 --> 00:32:24.842
That's what London is.

00:32:24.842 --> 00:32:26.177
I got to fluctuate it.

00:32:26.177 --> 00:32:29.847
So you can imagine now,
why, if it's heavier,

00:32:29.847 --> 00:32:32.817
and those electrons are further
out, they're more polarizable.

00:32:32.817 --> 00:32:33.384
Right.

00:32:33.384 --> 00:32:34.519
So let's write that down.

00:32:34.519 --> 00:32:35.019
Right.

00:32:35.019 --> 00:32:39.857
So the London-- so we're going
to get a stronger London--

00:32:43.428 --> 00:32:44.329
two reasons.

00:32:44.329 --> 00:32:51.803
One, heavier atom, which
really, in this case,

00:32:51.803 --> 00:32:53.504
right, it's heavier,
but it really

00:32:53.504 --> 00:32:55.807
just means more polarizable.

00:32:55.807 --> 00:32:58.977
If it's more polarizable,
that's the key.

00:32:58.977 --> 00:33:05.450
That's the chemistry key, more
polarizable, more polarizable

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:06.851
electron cloud.

00:33:06.851 --> 00:33:07.552
Write it all out.

00:33:10.188 --> 00:33:13.057
But the other thing that you
can imagine with London--

00:33:13.057 --> 00:33:14.592
right, so that
explains that trend.

00:33:14.592 --> 00:33:16.594
The other thing that you
can imagine with London

00:33:16.594 --> 00:33:19.964
is that these interactions,
these fluctuations, they're

00:33:19.964 --> 00:33:22.200
happening all over the place.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:25.503
And the more contact I have--

00:33:25.503 --> 00:33:30.808
the more places in a molecule
I have to induce this,

00:33:30.808 --> 00:33:34.012
to have this fluctuation happen,
the stronger these forces

00:33:34.012 --> 00:33:35.613
could be.

00:33:35.613 --> 00:33:37.181
And this is exactly what we see.

00:33:37.181 --> 00:33:38.282
Look at this trend.

00:33:38.282 --> 00:33:40.818
Methane, ethane,
propane, and butane.

00:33:40.818 --> 00:33:42.887
All I've done is I've
added another carbon atom.

00:33:42.887 --> 00:33:44.455
Oh, we're going to get to these.

00:33:44.455 --> 00:33:46.657
We'll talk a little
bit about Orgel later.

00:33:46.657 --> 00:33:48.693
But for now, look at the
boiling points of those.

00:33:48.693 --> 00:33:52.997
Again, boiling point being a
proxy for the bonding strength.

00:33:52.997 --> 00:33:55.066
What bond could they have?

00:33:55.066 --> 00:33:58.636
None of those, only this.

00:33:58.636 --> 00:33:59.570
Ha.

00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:01.239
I got excited.

00:34:01.239 --> 00:34:04.542
Only in London, because look,
they're non-polar molecules.

00:34:04.542 --> 00:34:05.810
They've got no charge on them.

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:07.845
This is the only way they
can talk to each other.

00:34:07.845 --> 00:34:09.914
But look at how much
they talk to each other.

00:34:09.914 --> 00:34:10.614
Now, why?

00:34:10.614 --> 00:34:12.050
Because of the
second thing, right.

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:14.485
Which is that the
greater the surface,

00:34:14.485 --> 00:34:16.154
the greater the surface.

00:34:16.154 --> 00:34:17.155
You can have more.

00:34:17.155 --> 00:34:19.524
The more surface you have--

00:34:19.524 --> 00:34:27.364
the greater surface,
more contact area,

00:34:27.364 --> 00:34:28.632
more contact area.

00:34:31.402 --> 00:34:33.304
And that's how this bond--

00:34:33.304 --> 00:34:34.972
that's how this thing works.

00:34:34.972 --> 00:34:40.844
Because as long as I've got
more surfaces see each other,

00:34:40.844 --> 00:34:43.246
then there can be more
fluctuations, right,

00:34:43.246 --> 00:34:45.583
that are happening, that
then induce the other one

00:34:45.583 --> 00:34:47.652
to have a fluctuation,
that then create

00:34:47.652 --> 00:34:51.255
a little moment of
bonding, that dramatically

00:34:51.255 --> 00:34:52.223
changes the properties.

00:34:52.223 --> 00:34:52.956
And look at this.

00:34:52.956 --> 00:34:56.127
This is a little bit small,
but, you know, these are the--

00:34:56.127 --> 00:34:58.396
so let's go all the way, right.

00:34:58.396 --> 00:35:00.865
CH4, OK.

00:35:00.865 --> 00:35:02.467
Let's go all the way,
and look at this.

00:35:02.467 --> 00:35:06.270
It determines when you have
a gas at room temperature

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:10.174
or when you have a liquid
at room temperature.

00:35:10.174 --> 00:35:13.878
Boy, is that important for
how we use these molecules.

00:35:13.878 --> 00:35:14.378
Right.

00:35:14.378 --> 00:35:16.314
Boy, is that important.

00:35:16.314 --> 00:35:18.182
It's all London.

00:35:18.182 --> 00:35:19.417
there's no other option.

00:35:19.417 --> 00:35:19.917
Right.

00:35:19.917 --> 00:35:22.053
But that's what's
dictating this trend.

00:35:22.053 --> 00:35:25.957
It's even more mind-blowing
than that, because look at this.

00:35:25.957 --> 00:35:31.095
This is one molecule
that's identical.

00:35:31.095 --> 00:35:31.963
This is C5.

00:35:31.963 --> 00:35:36.601
This is 5 atoms of
carbon, n-Pentane.

00:35:36.601 --> 00:35:40.004
Same exact grams per mole,
because it's the same chemical

00:35:40.004 --> 00:35:40.505
formula.

00:35:40.505 --> 00:35:44.308
But look here, 36.1 degrees
C is the boiling point,

00:35:44.308 --> 00:35:45.710
and here it's 9.5.

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:46.377
Why?

00:35:46.377 --> 00:35:48.379
It's all this.

00:35:48.379 --> 00:35:50.181
This is not the ground state.

00:35:50.181 --> 00:35:51.716
The most stable
structure is here.

00:35:51.716 --> 00:35:54.452
But there is a version
that's called neopentane.

00:35:54.452 --> 00:35:55.720
It's like a nickname for it.

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:59.257
There's a version of
it that you can make

00:35:59.257 --> 00:36:01.526
that has a different shape.

00:36:01.526 --> 00:36:05.663
And because this shape cannot
come into contact with itself,

00:36:05.663 --> 00:36:07.798
look at this, you can
see it from the red line.

00:36:07.798 --> 00:36:09.534
It's coming into
contact with itself.

00:36:09.534 --> 00:36:13.404
In the liquid or gas, it can't
have as much London forces,

00:36:13.404 --> 00:36:15.239
London potential.

00:36:15.239 --> 00:36:19.043
So it cannot bind as strongly,
and therefore the boiling point

00:36:19.043 --> 00:36:21.012
is a lot lower.

00:36:21.012 --> 00:36:22.780
It's a lot lower.

00:36:22.780 --> 00:36:27.385
Now, there's a little
bit of naming confusion.

00:36:27.385 --> 00:36:28.986
I want to clear this up.

00:36:28.986 --> 00:36:30.288
Oh, boy.

00:36:30.288 --> 00:36:31.556
I have nowhere to go here.

00:36:31.556 --> 00:36:35.059
So I'm going to get rid
of London for a second.

00:36:35.059 --> 00:36:38.229
And I want to make
something very clear

00:36:38.229 --> 00:36:42.433
that there's another
word that's used,

00:36:42.433 --> 00:36:44.435
which is called Van der Waals.

00:36:44.435 --> 00:36:44.936
OK.

00:36:44.936 --> 00:36:50.708
Now, this ability of a
fluctuation to happen,

00:36:50.708 --> 00:36:53.110
which then induces
a fluctuation, that

00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:56.581
can happen no matter
what molecule you have.

00:36:56.581 --> 00:36:57.215
It can happen.

00:36:57.215 --> 00:36:57.949
And it does.

00:36:57.949 --> 00:36:59.650
It's always there.

00:36:59.650 --> 00:37:00.184
All right.

00:37:00.184 --> 00:37:08.926
So all molecules, whether
they are polar or non-polar--

00:37:08.926 --> 00:37:18.970
polar or non-polar, all
molecules are attracted

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:32.049
by London plus any other
attractive forces--

00:37:32.049 --> 00:37:35.486
any other attractive
forces present--

00:37:42.059 --> 00:37:42.927
forces present.

00:37:42.927 --> 00:37:43.427
OK.

00:37:43.427 --> 00:37:45.830
So London is always there.

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:54.672
Now, people interchangeably
use the term Van der Waals.

00:37:54.672 --> 00:37:58.809
And what Van der
Waals encompasses

00:37:58.809 --> 00:38:02.246
is more than London.

00:38:02.246 --> 00:38:06.117
Van der Waals, what you will
sometimes see in textbooks,

00:38:06.117 --> 00:38:08.085
is the Van der Waals force.

00:38:10.921 --> 00:38:11.422
OK.

00:38:11.422 --> 00:38:20.231
So that is any or
all added together.

00:38:20.231 --> 00:38:26.504
This is the combination of these
weak forces of dipole-dipole--

00:38:26.504 --> 00:38:27.605
dipole-dipole.

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:41.619
Let's see, dipole-induced
dipole, and London.

00:38:45.389 --> 00:38:50.361
Those taken together
you will sometimes

00:38:50.361 --> 00:38:55.199
see referred to as Van der
Waals forces, or weak forces.

00:38:55.199 --> 00:38:56.967
But we know more than that.

00:38:56.967 --> 00:38:58.769
We know the distinction.

00:38:58.769 --> 00:38:59.937
Right.

00:38:59.937 --> 00:39:06.544
We know that London started like
this and then went like this.

00:39:10.948 --> 00:39:12.383
Ah!

00:39:12.383 --> 00:39:13.351
Really bad.

00:39:13.351 --> 00:39:14.719
Gesundheit.

00:39:14.719 --> 00:39:15.219
Right.

00:39:15.219 --> 00:39:17.588
We know that it
started with non-polar,

00:39:17.588 --> 00:39:18.856
and that there's a difference.

00:39:18.856 --> 00:39:23.227
And so this is all about the
polarizable electron cloud.

00:39:23.227 --> 00:39:27.832
Polarizable electron
cloud, that's all you got.

00:39:27.832 --> 00:39:28.566
Right.

00:39:28.566 --> 00:39:34.739
And this can be really
small, 0.5 to 40.

00:39:34.739 --> 00:39:35.239
Oh, yeah.

00:39:35.239 --> 00:39:40.444
Let's just keep with
chlorine 2, chlorine 2.

00:39:40.444 --> 00:39:43.614
Two non-polar molecules,
London dispersion,

00:39:43.614 --> 00:39:46.083
and this also has
a dependence of--

00:39:46.083 --> 00:39:48.552
in terms of the distance--
of 1 over r to the sixth.

00:39:48.552 --> 00:39:51.055
Now, in case you
think that's small,

00:39:51.055 --> 00:39:53.557
I want to show you a little
video of why this matters.

00:39:53.557 --> 00:39:57.361
So weak forces-- now, weak
forces are really strong.

00:39:57.361 --> 00:40:00.865
We know this because of
"Mission: Impossible".

00:40:00.865 --> 00:40:04.435
We know this also because
of our own students.

00:40:04.435 --> 00:40:06.737
There was one of my
former students, Rory,

00:40:06.737 --> 00:40:10.040
and he won all sorts
of prizes, because he

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:12.076
was trying to be like a gecko.

00:40:12.076 --> 00:40:14.912
And so he made gloves that
were like gecko gloves,

00:40:14.912 --> 00:40:19.350
and much to the delight of
the facilities people at MIT,

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:23.821
he actually climbed one of the
buildings using his gloves.

00:40:23.821 --> 00:40:28.225
In case you are wondering, it
wasn't actually their delight.

00:40:28.225 --> 00:40:30.227
But really cool stuff.

00:40:30.227 --> 00:40:31.028
So let me show you.

00:40:31.028 --> 00:40:33.164
This is like a 30-second video.

00:40:33.164 --> 00:40:38.202
He found the answer in the sheer
number and design of the hairs

00:40:38.202 --> 00:40:40.271
on the geckos feet.

00:40:40.271 --> 00:40:42.540
Geckos have millions
of microscopic hairs

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:43.207
on their toes.

00:40:43.207 --> 00:40:45.376
And of course, we can't see
this with our naked eye,

00:40:45.376 --> 00:40:49.647
because each hair is only
1/10 the size of a human hair.

00:40:49.647 --> 00:40:52.149
And each of those hairs
branch down to billions

00:40:52.149 --> 00:40:54.652
of little split ends.

00:40:54.652 --> 00:40:57.154
And they can make such close
contact with the surface

00:40:57.154 --> 00:41:00.524
that weak intermolecular
forces can begin to add up

00:41:00.524 --> 00:41:01.659
to something really strong.

00:41:04.328 --> 00:41:07.698
Turns out geckos exploit
something called the Van der

00:41:07.698 --> 00:41:08.833
Waals force.

00:41:08.833 --> 00:41:09.600
No.

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:10.835
London, London.

00:41:10.835 --> 00:41:13.771
If you think of an atom
as a dancing couple,

00:41:13.771 --> 00:41:17.141
when you bring two atoms
into very close contact,

00:41:17.141 --> 00:41:21.612
part of one atom can get
attracted to part of the other.

00:41:21.612 --> 00:41:25.149
That very weak bond is
the Van der Waals force,

00:41:25.149 --> 00:41:27.718
and it sticks atoms together.

00:41:27.718 --> 00:41:29.687
Proximity is the key.

00:41:29.687 --> 00:41:32.490
But bringing two materials
that near each other

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:34.458
is harder than you'd think.

00:41:34.458 --> 00:41:35.659
All right.

00:41:35.659 --> 00:41:38.863
Now, obviously, that
got me very excited

00:41:38.863 --> 00:41:42.132
when I saw that, because
that is how I see atoms,

00:41:42.132 --> 00:41:43.934
and that is how I see electrons.

00:41:43.934 --> 00:41:46.971
And I saw this, and
I almost fell over.

00:41:46.971 --> 00:41:50.007
And of course, I played it,
and I know all those moves.

00:41:50.007 --> 00:41:51.475
I would suggest you guys--

00:41:51.475 --> 00:41:52.710
it's a Friday.

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:54.678
There's some good
moves in there.

00:41:54.678 --> 00:41:56.247
There's some good
moves in there.

00:41:56.247 --> 00:41:57.681
And you can take
this on your phone

00:41:57.681 --> 00:42:00.451
and just kind of work
it out at the club.

00:42:00.451 --> 00:42:01.819
And speaking of
the club, there's

00:42:01.819 --> 00:42:03.354
one more thing I've
got to teach you.

00:42:03.354 --> 00:42:04.989
Because it's that last line.

00:42:04.989 --> 00:42:07.658
It's that last line.

00:42:07.658 --> 00:42:13.330
See, if you go along a trend
like methane, silane, germane,

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:16.100
right, these are
non-polar molecules,

00:42:16.100 --> 00:42:18.669
and they're all
attracted to each other

00:42:18.669 --> 00:42:19.637
by these Van der Waals.

00:42:19.637 --> 00:42:21.772
And you can see that the
boiling point, remember,

00:42:21.772 --> 00:42:24.775
our proxy for the force, the
bonding strength, is going up.

00:42:24.775 --> 00:42:26.076
That's what you'd expect.

00:42:26.076 --> 00:42:27.745
That's because these
are getting larger,

00:42:27.745 --> 00:42:31.315
and there are going to be
more polarizable electrons.

00:42:31.315 --> 00:42:34.685
But look at what happens as
you go to other molecules.

00:42:34.685 --> 00:42:38.556
This is the expected
trend until you get here.

00:42:38.556 --> 00:42:39.690
Right.

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:43.794
NH3-- and that is because
of one last type of bond.

00:42:43.794 --> 00:42:46.397
That is because of something
called the hydrogen bond.

00:42:46.397 --> 00:42:50.267
It's a very unique kind of bond.

00:42:50.267 --> 00:42:55.406
And so a hydrogen bond is
our last intermolecular bond.

00:42:55.406 --> 00:42:59.944
Hydrogen bond, right.

00:42:59.944 --> 00:43:01.712
And it's a very
particular kind of bond.

00:43:01.712 --> 00:43:03.614
And it has to do is
something very particular

00:43:03.614 --> 00:43:05.749
that hydrogen does.

00:43:05.749 --> 00:43:06.951
Look at those boiling points.

00:43:06.951 --> 00:43:08.619
Look at the change.

00:43:08.619 --> 00:43:12.957
I mean, H2O is H2O because
of the hydrogen bonds.

00:43:12.957 --> 00:43:13.791
Right.

00:43:13.791 --> 00:43:18.529
Earth's operating conditions
are luckily right here.

00:43:18.529 --> 00:43:21.298
But this is only good
because of hydrogen bonds,

00:43:21.298 --> 00:43:24.001
and it's all the way up and
not all the way down there.

00:43:24.001 --> 00:43:25.269
So hydrogen bonds are unique.

00:43:25.269 --> 00:43:27.204
This is the
electronegativity scale,

00:43:27.204 --> 00:43:28.706
and there's something
very important

00:43:28.706 --> 00:43:30.274
that happens for
those three elements.

00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:35.245
Those are the three elements
that are in this trend.

00:43:35.245 --> 00:43:36.480
Those are the three elements.

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:39.249
See, nitrogen, chlorine,
and oxygen, those

00:43:39.249 --> 00:43:40.684
are the three elements here.

00:43:40.684 --> 00:43:42.252
And what's important
about them is

00:43:42.252 --> 00:43:45.255
that they have such a
huge electronegativity.

00:43:45.255 --> 00:43:50.260
And so in a hydrogen bond,
what's going to happen--

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:51.962
what's going to
happen in a hydrogen

00:43:51.962 --> 00:43:55.065
is they're really going
to pull a lot of charge

00:43:55.065 --> 00:43:58.168
from the hydrogen.

00:43:58.168 --> 00:44:03.841
So you have a very high
positive charge on the hydrogen,

00:44:03.841 --> 00:44:05.976
but there's something
else that happens.

00:44:05.976 --> 00:44:09.046
For hydrogen bonding
to occur, you see,

00:44:09.046 --> 00:44:13.450
it's the H with say
fluorine, and you

00:44:13.450 --> 00:44:17.454
have a delta plus and a delta
minus, and this is high.

00:44:17.454 --> 00:44:18.856
This is very high.

00:44:18.856 --> 00:44:19.590
All right.

00:44:19.590 --> 00:44:23.527
But you also have a
lone pair somewhere.

00:44:23.527 --> 00:44:25.129
You've also got a
lone pair somewhere.

00:44:25.129 --> 00:44:29.033
And we know that we have
that on the fluorine, right.

00:44:29.033 --> 00:44:31.268
We know that, and
we've got those two.

00:44:31.268 --> 00:44:34.471
And the bond that a
hydrogen bond represents

00:44:34.471 --> 00:44:38.242
is the fact that I've done two
things with the same molecule.

00:44:38.242 --> 00:44:40.310
I've taken a lot of
charge off of hydrogen

00:44:40.310 --> 00:44:43.347
and created a very strong
delta plus on the hydrogen.

00:44:43.347 --> 00:44:46.116
But I've also got a
lone pair somewhere

00:44:46.116 --> 00:44:48.052
that that can bond to.

00:44:48.052 --> 00:44:51.922
And when I have those two
things, which I really

00:44:51.922 --> 00:44:53.924
have for these three
elements, right.

00:44:53.924 --> 00:44:56.860
When I have those two things, I
can make this very special kind

00:44:56.860 --> 00:44:58.262
of bond.

00:44:58.262 --> 00:44:59.930
Now, this is what it looks like.

00:44:59.930 --> 00:45:00.431
Right.

00:45:00.431 --> 00:45:05.836
So there's an H2O molecule, and
I want to make a point here.

00:45:05.836 --> 00:45:08.539
There's the covalent
bond between the oxygen

00:45:08.539 --> 00:45:10.674
and hydrogen. There's
the delta plus.

00:45:10.674 --> 00:45:13.243
And there's that hydrogen
bond right there.

00:45:13.243 --> 00:45:14.044
Mm.

00:45:14.044 --> 00:45:14.845
Mm.

00:45:14.845 --> 00:45:15.512
There.

00:45:15.512 --> 00:45:16.013
OK.

00:45:16.013 --> 00:45:18.782
Hydrogen bond that forms
between the delta plus

00:45:18.782 --> 00:45:19.917
and the lone pair.

00:45:19.917 --> 00:45:23.153
Now, you can see why
water is so special.

00:45:23.153 --> 00:45:24.054
You can see.

00:45:24.054 --> 00:45:27.124
Because unlike
NH3 or HF, you can

00:45:27.124 --> 00:45:29.059
see where water had
the biggest kick

00:45:29.059 --> 00:45:31.261
up from what would be expected.

00:45:31.261 --> 00:45:36.366
Because unlike NH3 or HF, you
have exactly the right balance.

00:45:36.366 --> 00:45:39.536
I've created two of these
very strong delta pluses,

00:45:39.536 --> 00:45:42.239
and I've got two lone pairs.

00:45:42.239 --> 00:45:43.107
It all all works out.

00:45:43.107 --> 00:45:45.342
There's no extra lone
pairs, like here.

00:45:45.342 --> 00:45:50.447
And all these extra lone
pairs, right, that don't--

00:45:50.447 --> 00:45:52.983
you know, each molecule
only has one hydrogen. Here,

00:45:52.983 --> 00:45:54.384
it's two and two.

00:45:54.384 --> 00:45:57.588
That's why water is so special.

00:45:57.588 --> 00:45:59.123
This bond is so important.

00:45:59.123 --> 00:46:01.125
I know you've probably
heard about it for water,

00:46:01.125 --> 00:46:04.795
but here's the example I want
to give you for the club,

00:46:04.795 --> 00:46:07.965
because some of you might
be able to drink ethanol,

00:46:07.965 --> 00:46:12.936
only if you're at the right
age, please, of course.

00:46:12.936 --> 00:46:15.272
This is what's in
alcohol, right.

00:46:15.272 --> 00:46:16.473
This is the ethanol molecule.

00:46:16.473 --> 00:46:18.809
And it has a boiling
point of 78.5 degrees.

00:46:18.809 --> 00:46:21.445
Again, that tells you something
about the bond strength

00:46:21.445 --> 00:46:23.680
of this molecule to
another molecule.

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:25.282
But look at this.

00:46:25.282 --> 00:46:28.752
That's an oxygen
with a hydrogen.

00:46:28.752 --> 00:46:31.121
This took a lot of
charge from the hydrogen,

00:46:31.121 --> 00:46:32.856
right, so you've got
a strong delta plus.

00:46:32.856 --> 00:46:35.159
But you've got those
two lone pairs there

00:46:35.159 --> 00:46:37.961
ready to bond to the
other ethanol molecule.

00:46:37.961 --> 00:46:39.129
But look what happens.

00:46:39.129 --> 00:46:42.432
Methoxymethane is
a gas at room temp.

00:46:42.432 --> 00:46:43.634
It's boiled off.

00:46:43.634 --> 00:46:46.370
It's the exact same
chemical formula.

00:46:46.370 --> 00:46:47.938
It's the exact same
chemical formula.

00:46:47.938 --> 00:46:50.440
All I've done is
shift the oxygen in,

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:52.709
and so now there's
no hydrogen that's

00:46:52.709 --> 00:46:55.145
had that big of a delta on it.

00:46:55.145 --> 00:46:56.446
So it cannot hydrogen bond.

00:46:56.446 --> 00:46:58.248
It can London.

00:46:58.248 --> 00:46:58.982
You know.

00:46:58.982 --> 00:47:01.218
But it can't hydrogen. It can't.

00:47:01.218 --> 00:47:02.986
Because the oxygen
has the lone pairs.

00:47:02.986 --> 00:47:05.522
But there's no hydrogens that
have that strong of a delta.

00:47:05.522 --> 00:47:06.990
So when you're at
the club tonight,

00:47:06.990 --> 00:47:08.826
trying out those moves,
go up to somebody

00:47:08.826 --> 00:47:10.527
and say, it's a really
good thing that's

00:47:10.527 --> 00:47:13.664
not methoxymethane, isn't it.

00:47:13.664 --> 00:47:15.032
Really good thing.

00:47:15.032 --> 00:47:16.500
And tell them about
hydrogen bonds.

00:47:16.500 --> 00:47:18.502
Have a great weekend.

